            {
                id infra.prepared-api
                title "Design: typed prepared-statement API, or confirm the statement cache suffices"
                kind @feat
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-09-03T18:46:56.709955Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Operator question (2026-09-03): is there any actual PostgreSQL benefit to a first-class Statement type? Established baseline: the old SeaORM Statement was multi-backend vestige, correctly deleted; ConnectionTrait is generic over tokio-postgres ToStatement so a prepared tokio_postgres::Statement already passes; pgorm-pool's per-connection statement cache already gives prepare-once-reuse keyed by SQL text. The open design question is a TYPED surface — e.g. conn.prepare::<M>(sql) returning a handle that carries model/param types from Describe metadata. Core constraint to weigh: tokio-postgres Statements are per-connection objects, so a typed handle either pins a pooled connection (fights the pool) or wraps the cache lookup (types without a held handle). Possible sweeteners: parameter type-checking at prepare time, avoiding re-describe, pipelining, and pairing with the sql! macro (libpg_query already validates the text; a typed handle could carry the inferred shape). Deliverable is a design verdict first — build only if the value beats the cache's transparency; \"the cache suffices\" is an acceptable outcome, recorded as a decision."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-09-03T20:24:31.622416Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Probe verdict: the node's premise was false — the statement cache does NOT give prepare-once-reuse today, because nothing calls it: all three ConnectionTrait impls delegate straight to tokio_postgres (fresh server-side statement per call, Drop queues Close+Sync), and DatabaseConnection's pooled object is crate-private so no caller can reach prepare_cached either. Measured on a counting wire proxy: every ORM query pays 3 syncs (Parse+Describe / Bind+Execute / Close) where 1 suffices; routing through the existing cache is 2.30x on real ORM queries at 4ms RTT; cache misses are linear in pool size, not query count. A typed handle buys ~10% over cache routing and starves the pool (measured 502ms timeout at max_size=2 with two pinned handles); ConnectionTrait already accepts a foreign-connection Statement that fails at runtime with SQLSTATE 26000 (recorded as a representable-invalid-state candidate). Verdict (b): wire the six extended-protocol ConnectionTrait methods through prepare_cached on the existing SqlText seam (Statement passes through untouched); evict-and-retry-once on SQLSTATE 0A000 (the one hazard reuse introduces — probed: search_path and table-recreation are handled by PG's plan cache, only result-type-changing DDL trips it); bound the cache (25 IN-list arities = 25 entries, and Clean recycling keeps them); optionally a verify::<M> utility from Statement::columns() — the only thing that catches a wrong FromQueryResult target when the query returns zero rows, which today decodes Ok(vec![]) and ships. Four implementing nodes sketched in the agent report; AKM record at [dec:pgorm:prepared-statement-reuse]. Spike archived on the node's branch."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-09-03T20:25:06.421353Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id infra.statement-cache
                title "Route ConnectionTrait through the statement cache: evict-once on 0A000, bounded keys"
                kind @feat
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-09-03T20:37:59.772606Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Implements [dec:pgorm:prepared-statement-reuse] (probe evidence on spike/prepared-api, prototype in its src/main.rs): the six extended-protocol ConnectionTrait methods resolve SqlText::sql_text() through the connection's existing StatementCache (prepare_cached); a passed tokio_postgres::Statement (sql_text() None) goes through untouched. Three concerns in one node: (1) routing — three impls in src/database/db_connection.rs; (2) invalidation — on SQLSTATE 0A000 (cached plan must not change result type, the one hazard reuse introduces; probed: search_path changes and table recreation are re-planned fine by PG) evict the key via StatementCaches::remove and re-prepare ONCE, second failure surfaces; (3) bound — capacity cap with eviction plus a ManagerConfig opt-out (key space is unbounded: 25 IN-list arities = 25 entries; Clean recycling deliberately keeps entries). Measured 2.30x on real ORM queries at 4ms RTT; misses linear in pool size not query count. Spec: bump conn.pool.statement-cache (its cache-is-opt-in paragraph becomes false) and conn.pool.conn-trait; new rules for invalidate + bound. Note eq-any's paved road exists partly for this: eq_any keeps one SQL text across list cardinalities."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-09-03T21:23:45.108082Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id infra.statement-verify
                title "Prepare-time verification of a decode target against Statement::columns()"
                kind @feat
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-09-03T20:38:02.624426Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "From [dec:pgorm:prepared-statement-reuse]'s probe 2: a FromQueryResult target with a wrong column type or naming a nonexistent column decodes Ok(vec![]) whenever the query matches zero rows — the bug ships and detonates when data appears. Statement::columns() + FromSql::accepts catches all of it at prepare time regardless of row count, and params() covers parameter mismatches. Build verify as a utility (not a handle type): verify::<M>(conn, sql) -> Result<(), Error>. Needs a column-shape reflection hook on FromQueryResult (name + a type-acceptance check per column), derive-generated in pgorm-macros. Fallible boundary returns Error per [dec:pgorm:no-panic]."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-09-03T21:19:44.577055Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
