            {
                id polish.epoch-return
                title "Stale/Review epoch bookkeeping: how does a drifted node return to Done?"
                kind @chore
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-09-04T14:41:02.371749Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Eight pre-campaign nodes sit in derived Stale/Review after this session's rule bumps: sql.ast, sql.ddl (stale since the campaign), plus the 2026-09-04 sync's batch — orm.binding-types, infra.tx.closure, codegen.generate, decide.migration (Stale), infra.tx.tests, decide.cli (Review). All their rules are 100% covered/tested at current versions with zero stale annotations — the drift IS the re-verification landing after their Done stamps. Attempted and failed: a re-acknowledging chore commit addressing sql.ast+sql.ddl (12f8d400) did not flip them, so the seen-version stamp must come from somewhere else (per-rule nspec-id trailers? only commits whose staged delta touches the rules' files?). Deliverable: understand the actual epoch-return mechanism (read nplan's docs/spec on reconciliation or probe the daemon), then clear all eight properly — no status hand-editing, statuses are derived."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-09-05T13:38:55.58017Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Mechanism found (investigation agent, evidence from nplan's own source + tests at /Users/brendan/git/necessary/nplan): a node's seen_version lives in plan/.state.styx impl_evidence[].rule_versions_seen, compared against the top-level rule_versions block; nplan_sync rebuilds a node's evidence ONLY when the node is named by a commit that also bears >=1 rule trailer (sync.rs:491 bears_rule_claims short-circuit; trailers derive solely from staged annotation diffs, scaffold/trailers.rs) — which is exactly why the id-only re-acknowledge commit 12f8d400 did nothing (pinned by nplan's own id_only_commit_preserves_carried_evidence test). The Review pair (decide.cli, infra.tx.tests) is pure dep propagation via inv.trans-stale+2 and clears when their Stale deps do. Resolution: piggyback the six drifted ids onto the next annotation-touching commit (ergo.fluent-select's), then nplan_sync. Commit-type caution honored: refactor leaves delivered empty so no delivery gate fires on the mixed node kinds. UPSTREAM (nplan) findings for the operator: (1) spec/impl divergence — lc.stale+3 and lc.no-reopen+2 promise clearing on \"a newly-landed commit with coverage at current version\" with no trailer requirement; the bears_rule_claims gate (justified by ix.git-walk-bound+2, a walk-cost rule) makes a node whose rules were bumped by ANOTHER node's work unable to return to Done self-contained — a missing re-acknowledge primitive. (2) A loophole exists (remove rules from implements, sync, re-add, sync — flips Done with no source diff, manufacturing false evidence); should be closed when the primitive is added."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-09-05T13:39:00.351535Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
